1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,060
Meet Dana.

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:03,785
She runs a shift at a warehouse

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:04,960
in Ohio.

4
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:05,212
This spring,

5
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:05,890
the robots arrived.

6
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:07,510
They don't take a salary.

7
00:00:07,510 --> 00:00:10,370
They don't take vacations.

8
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:12,370
They never ask for a raise.

9
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,028
And the warehouse is making more money

10
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:15,080
than ever.

11
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:16,930
So Dana has a question.

12
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:18,430
If the robots do the work,

13
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:19,980
who gets the money?

14
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:21,260
Here's a clue.

15
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:22,380
In America,

16
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:24,193
the share of business output paid to

17
00:00:24,193 --> 00:00:26,006
workers has just hit the lowest level

18
00:00:26,006 --> 00:00:27,150
on record.

19
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:29,254
So the real question about AI may

20
00:00:29,254 --> 00:00:29,931
not be,

21
00:00:29,931 --> 00:00:31,570
will I keep my job?

22
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,357
It may be,

23
00:00:32,357 --> 00:00:34,975
do I own any of the machines?

24
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:48,728
We followed the robots in Could AI

25
00:00:48,728 --> 00:00:51,709
Make American Factories Cheaper Than

26
00:00:51,709 --> 00:00:52,495
China's,

27
00:00:52,495 --> 00:00:54,664
and in Could AI Bring Europe Back

28
00:00:54,664 --> 00:00:55,967
to Power.

29
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:57,719
Now let's follow the money,

30
00:00:57,719 --> 00:00:59,207
through Dana's eyes.

31
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:01,030
Let me draw it.

32
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:02,985
Picture a hundred-dollar bill that stands

33
00:01:02,985 --> 00:01:05,540
for everything America earns.

34
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,267
A slice goes to the people who

35
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:08,100
do the work,

36
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:09,870
as pay and benefits.

37
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:11,544
A slice goes to the owners,

38
00:01:11,544 --> 00:01:12,750
as profit.

39
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:15,418
The rest covers machines wearing out,

40
00:01:15,418 --> 00:01:17,487
taxes and interest.

41
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:20,181
When Dana's dad started on the loading

42
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:20,643
dock,

43
00:01:20,643 --> 00:01:22,320
back in the seventies,

44
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:24,751
workers got about fifty-eight dollars of

45
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:25,837
that bill.

46
00:01:26,083 --> 00:01:26,800
Today,

47
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,883
they get about fifty-one.

48
00:01:28,893 --> 00:01:30,145
And the owners' slice,

49
00:01:30,145 --> 00:01:31,397
profit after tax,

50
00:01:31,397 --> 00:01:33,058
has roughly doubled.

51
00:01:33,317 --> 00:01:36,417
A few dollars doesn't sound like much.

52
00:01:36,417 --> 00:01:37,817
But this bill stands for the whole

53
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:39,817
American economy.

54
00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:41,156
By our rough math,

55
00:01:41,156 --> 00:01:42,869
that gap is worth more than two

56
00:01:42,869 --> 00:01:44,927
trillion dollars a year.

57
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:46,207
Not stolen.

58
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:48,385
Just a split that moved.

59
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:50,626
The Labor Department tracks the same thing

60
00:01:50,626 --> 00:01:52,610
for private businesses.

61
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,428
For decades after the war,

62
00:01:54,428 --> 00:01:56,590
workers' share barely budged.

63
00:01:56,590 --> 00:01:58,880
Then it started to slide.

64
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:01,782
Today it's the lowest on record.

65
00:02:02,017 --> 00:02:03,117
Why?

66
00:02:03,117 --> 00:02:05,207
Economists still argue.

67
00:02:05,207 --> 00:02:05,974
Global trade,

68
00:02:05,974 --> 00:02:07,726
a few superstar companies,

69
00:02:07,726 --> 00:02:09,807
and automation all get blamed.

70
00:02:09,807 --> 00:02:11,607
But here's what a robot adds.

71
00:02:11,607 --> 00:02:13,357
A robot is capital.

72
00:02:13,357 --> 00:02:14,902
When a machine does a task a

73
00:02:14,902 --> 00:02:16,235
person used to do,

74
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:17,974
the money that paid that person now

75
00:02:17,974 --> 00:02:20,119
goes to whoever owns the machine.

76
00:02:20,383 --> 00:02:22,783
Dana gets it right away.

77
00:02:22,783 --> 00:02:24,790
Her warehouse didn't fire anyone this

78
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:25,543
year.

79
00:02:25,543 --> 00:02:27,334
It just stopped hiring for the night

80
00:02:27,334 --> 00:02:28,123
shift.

81
00:02:28,123 --> 00:02:30,123
The robots took those hours.

82
00:02:30,123 --> 00:02:31,341
And the money those hours used to

83
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:32,173
earn didn't vanish.

84
00:02:32,173 --> 00:02:33,581
It went somewhere.

85
00:02:38,017 --> 00:02:40,867
So Dana asks the obvious question.

86
00:02:40,867 --> 00:02:42,687
Who owns the machines?

87
00:02:42,687 --> 00:02:43,223
Mostly,

88
00:02:43,223 --> 00:02:45,027
whoever owns the company.

89
00:02:45,027 --> 00:02:45,625
In other words,

90
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:45,997
stocks.

91
00:02:45,997 --> 00:02:48,414
So let's line up every household in

92
00:02:48,414 --> 00:02:49,490
America,

93
00:02:49,490 --> 00:02:51,445
from the least wealthy to the most,

94
00:02:51,445 --> 00:02:53,423
and share out a hundred dollars' worth

95
00:02:53,423 --> 00:02:55,121
of the stock they own.

96
00:02:55,383 --> 00:02:57,693
The whole bottom half of the country,

97
00:02:57,693 --> 00:02:58,731
all together,

98
00:02:58,731 --> 00:03:00,603
gets less than a dollar.

99
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:03,390
And the richest tenth of households?

100
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:05,280
They get almost ninety.

101
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,439
Nearly the whole pile.

102
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:09,046
Dana's neighbor Milo,

103
00:03:09,046 --> 00:03:10,607
a retired bus driver,

104
00:03:10,607 --> 00:03:13,183
has a retirement account from work.

105
00:03:13,323 --> 00:03:16,005
Accounts like his are spread wider than

106
00:03:16,005 --> 00:03:16,873
stocks.

107
00:03:16,873 --> 00:03:17,952
But even there,

108
00:03:17,952 --> 00:03:21,195
the poorer households hold only a sliver.

109
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:23,530
Now connect the dots.

110
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:26,073
If robots move money from paychecks to

111
00:03:26,073 --> 00:03:26,811
profits,

112
00:03:26,811 --> 00:03:28,820
the money climbs this ladder,

113
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:31,200
to whoever holds the shares.

114
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:32,530
For generations,

115
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:35,350
getting ahead meant getting a better job.

116
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:37,609
When machines do more of the work,

117
00:03:37,609 --> 00:03:39,644
owning a piece of them could matter

118
00:03:39,644 --> 00:03:40,922
just as much.

119
00:03:45,383 --> 00:03:46,325
And right now,

120
00:03:46,325 --> 00:03:49,222
the biggest machines in history are going

121
00:03:49,222 --> 00:03:49,913
up.

122
00:03:49,913 --> 00:03:52,023
Data centers for AI.

123
00:03:52,023 --> 00:03:52,973
Amazon,

124
00:03:52,973 --> 00:03:53,924
Alphabet,

125
00:03:53,924 --> 00:03:56,360
Microsoft and Meta spent more than six

126
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:58,797
hundred billion dollars on buildings and

127
00:03:58,797 --> 00:04:00,722
equipment in the past year,

128
00:04:00,722 --> 00:04:02,053
by our sum.

129
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:04,248
Much of that money lands at the

130
00:04:04,248 --> 00:04:06,537
company selling the chips.

131
00:04:06,587 --> 00:04:08,061
In its latest quarter,

132
00:04:08,061 --> 00:04:10,411
Nvidia kept most of every dollar of

133
00:04:10,411 --> 00:04:12,080
sales as profit.

134
00:04:12,317 --> 00:04:14,067
Let me draw the loop.

135
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:16,142
A business pays for AI instead of

136
00:04:16,142 --> 00:04:17,147
hiring.

137
00:04:17,147 --> 00:04:19,587
That money goes to a cloud company.

138
00:04:19,587 --> 00:04:21,685
The cloud company buys chips and builds

139
00:04:21,685 --> 00:04:23,187
data centers.

140
00:04:23,187 --> 00:04:26,676
Profits flow to the shareholders of both.

141
00:04:26,917 --> 00:04:29,787
Now notice who's missing from the loop.

142
00:04:29,787 --> 00:04:32,227
The worker whose task was automated.

143
00:04:32,267 --> 00:04:35,317
Unless that worker also owns shares.

144
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:37,558
Dana looks at the loop and sees

145
00:04:37,558 --> 00:04:39,343
herself outside it.

146
00:04:39,343 --> 00:04:41,373
Her pay comes from the warehouse.

147
00:04:41,373 --> 00:04:43,173
The robots come from someone else's

148
00:04:43,173 --> 00:04:43,953
factory,

149
00:04:43,953 --> 00:04:46,353
and they run on someone else's cloud.

150
00:04:46,473 --> 00:04:49,023
Every step of that chain has owners.

151
00:04:49,073 --> 00:04:50,813
She isn't one of them.

152
00:04:51,003 --> 00:04:52,137
Yet.

153
00:04:52,383 --> 00:04:53,457
But it's a bet,

154
00:04:53,457 --> 00:04:55,123
not a guarantee.

155
00:04:55,123 --> 00:04:56,815
Some of this spending may never pay

156
00:04:56,815 --> 00:04:57,603
off.

157
00:04:57,643 --> 00:04:58,842
Over the past year,

158
00:04:58,842 --> 00:05:01,077
Amazon spent more cash than its business

159
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:02,083
brought in.

160
00:05:02,273 --> 00:05:03,933
Owners get the upside.

161
00:05:03,933 --> 00:05:06,423
Owners also carry the risk.

162
00:05:10,883 --> 00:05:12,924
So is AI already moving money from

163
00:05:12,924 --> 00:05:15,153
people like Dana to the owners?

164
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:16,880
Let's separate what's been measured from

165
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,193
what's been predicted.

166
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:18,651
First,

167
00:05:18,651 --> 00:05:19,893
a finding.

168
00:05:19,893 --> 00:05:22,248
When a company gave its customer-support

169
00:05:22,248 --> 00:05:23,799
agents an AI assistant,

170
00:05:23,799 --> 00:05:25,545
they solved more problems per hour,

171
00:05:25,545 --> 00:05:26,507
and the newest,

172
00:05:26,507 --> 00:05:29,266
least experienced agents gained the most.

173
00:05:29,517 --> 00:05:30,393
In Denmark,

174
00:05:30,393 --> 00:05:32,415
researchers matched chatbot use with

175
00:05:32,415 --> 00:05:34,477
official payroll records.

176
00:05:34,477 --> 00:05:36,094
Since ChatGPT arrived,

177
00:05:36,094 --> 00:05:39,108
they've found no measurable effect on pay

178
00:05:39,108 --> 00:05:40,256
or hours.

179
00:05:40,517 --> 00:05:42,166
But in America,

180
00:05:42,166 --> 00:05:45,157
payroll data shows something to watch.

181
00:05:45,217 --> 00:05:47,291
Among the youngest workers in the jobs

182
00:05:47,291 --> 00:05:48,722
most exposed to AI,

183
00:05:48,722 --> 00:05:51,003
employment is almost a fifth below where

184
00:05:51,003 --> 00:05:52,847
it would otherwise be.

185
00:05:52,937 --> 00:05:55,647
Experienced workers show no such gap.

186
00:05:55,647 --> 00:05:58,333
The researchers call it an early warning,

187
00:05:58,333 --> 00:05:59,384
not proof.

188
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:02,660
Factory robots have a longer record.

189
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:05,574
Economists found that American towns that

190
00:06:05,574 --> 00:06:07,995
added more robots saw fewer jobs and

191
00:06:07,995 --> 00:06:09,805
slightly lower pay.

192
00:06:10,050 --> 00:06:12,490
Then there are forecasts.

193
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:15,662
MIT economist Daron Acemoglu predicts AI

194
00:06:15,662 --> 00:06:18,381
will give productivity only a small lift

195
00:06:18,381 --> 00:06:19,945
over the next decade,

196
00:06:19,945 --> 00:06:22,437
and widen the gap between what capital

197
00:06:22,437 --> 00:06:24,170
earns and what work earns.

198
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:25,280
That's a model,

199
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:27,030
not a measurement.

200
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:29,699
Forecasts differ widely.

201
00:06:34,150 --> 00:06:36,900
And this has happened before.

202
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:37,816
Britain,

203
00:06:37,816 --> 00:06:39,610
the age of steam.

204
00:06:39,610 --> 00:06:41,967
Machines multiplied what every worker

205
00:06:41,967 --> 00:06:42,960
could make.

206
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:45,240
Factory owners grew rich.

207
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:47,615
Workers' pay barely moved.

208
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:50,211
Economic historian Robert Allen measured

209
00:06:50,211 --> 00:06:50,830
it.

210
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:52,450
Let me draw his story.

211
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:54,610
Output per worker kept climbing.

212
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:56,550
Real wages stayed flat.

213
00:06:56,550 --> 00:06:58,360
The profit rate doubled.

214
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,374
Historians call it Engels' pause,

215
00:07:00,374 --> 00:07:01,935
after Friedrich Engels,

216
00:07:01,935 --> 00:07:04,424
who described the factory workers of

217
00:07:04,424 --> 00:07:05,703
Manchester.

218
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:06,335
Then,

219
00:07:06,335 --> 00:07:08,160
around the middle of the eighteen

220
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:08,748
hundreds,

221
00:07:08,748 --> 00:07:10,776
wages finally began to rise with

222
00:07:10,776 --> 00:07:12,390
productivity.

223
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:13,930
The pause ended.

224
00:07:13,930 --> 00:07:15,780
But it lasted decades.

225
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:17,769
For the workers who lived through it,

226
00:07:17,769 --> 00:07:19,776
eventually came too late.

227
00:07:20,017 --> 00:07:22,126
So are we living through a new

228
00:07:22,126 --> 00:07:23,377
Engels' pause?

229
00:07:23,377 --> 00:07:25,067
Nobody can say yet.

230
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:27,429
Workers' share of business output is at

231
00:07:27,429 --> 00:07:28,481
a record low,

232
00:07:28,481 --> 00:07:31,657
but AI has barely reached most workplaces.

233
00:07:31,747 --> 00:07:33,692
What history does show is that the

234
00:07:33,692 --> 00:07:35,184
pause wasn't automatic,

235
00:07:35,184 --> 00:07:37,297
and neither was the catch-up.

236
00:07:37,357 --> 00:07:38,857
It depends on new jobs,

237
00:07:38,857 --> 00:07:39,488
new skills,

238
00:07:39,488 --> 00:07:41,010
and who gets to own the new

239
00:07:41,010 --> 00:07:42,262
machines.

240
00:07:46,717 --> 00:07:47,197
Now,

241
00:07:47,197 --> 00:07:48,757
the other side.

242
00:07:48,757 --> 00:07:48,962
First,

243
00:07:48,962 --> 00:07:49,377
new jobs.

244
00:07:49,377 --> 00:07:52,043
A team led by economist David Autor

245
00:07:52,043 --> 00:07:54,487
found that most Americans today work in

246
00:07:54,487 --> 00:07:56,486
job specialties that didn't exist in

247
00:07:56,486 --> 00:07:58,037
nineteen forty.

248
00:07:58,037 --> 00:08:00,147
Machines take over tasks.

249
00:08:00,147 --> 00:08:02,648
New kinds of work keep appearing.

250
00:08:02,883 --> 00:08:03,492
Second,

251
00:08:03,492 --> 00:08:05,773
productivity can lift pay.

252
00:08:05,773 --> 00:08:07,393
When things get cheaper to make,

253
00:08:07,393 --> 00:08:08,773
people buy more.

254
00:08:08,773 --> 00:08:10,487
And workers who use AI well can

255
00:08:10,487 --> 00:08:12,323
become more valuable.

256
00:08:12,323 --> 00:08:13,091
Like Dana,

257
00:08:13,091 --> 00:08:15,069
who now runs the robot fleet.

258
00:08:15,317 --> 00:08:15,503
Third,

259
00:08:15,503 --> 00:08:16,237
skills still pay.

260
00:08:16,237 --> 00:08:18,772
A full-time worker with at least a

261
00:08:18,772 --> 00:08:22,151
bachelor's degree typically earns about

262
00:08:22,151 --> 00:08:24,404
three-quarters more than a high school

263
00:08:24,404 --> 00:08:25,567
graduate.

264
00:08:25,817 --> 00:08:26,760
And fourth,

265
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:29,637
policy can change the split.

266
00:08:29,707 --> 00:08:31,569
Some want to spread ownership wider,

267
00:08:31,569 --> 00:08:32,867
with employee ownership,

268
00:08:32,867 --> 00:08:33,789
accounts for kids,

269
00:08:33,789 --> 00:08:35,482
or public funds like Alaska's oil

270
00:08:35,482 --> 00:08:36,216
dividend,

271
00:08:36,216 --> 00:08:38,517
which we covered in another film.

272
00:08:38,517 --> 00:08:39,707
Others prefer training,

273
00:08:39,707 --> 00:08:41,432
or changing how work and capital are

274
00:08:41,432 --> 00:08:42,117
taxed.

275
00:08:42,117 --> 00:08:44,867
Others say markets will sort it out.

276
00:08:44,867 --> 00:08:47,257
Each has trade-offs.

277
00:08:47,317 --> 00:08:49,194
This film takes no side.

278
00:08:53,650 --> 00:08:56,700
So Dana asks the practical question.

279
00:08:56,750 --> 00:08:59,540
What can an ordinary person actually own?

280
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:00,470
Quick note.

281
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:01,695
This is education,

282
00:09:01,695 --> 00:09:03,200
not investment advice.

283
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,420
We're not telling anyone what to buy.

284
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:07,709
Most American families own at least some

285
00:09:07,709 --> 00:09:08,066
stock,

286
00:09:08,066 --> 00:09:10,540
often through a retirement account.

287
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:12,823
But among lower-income families,

288
00:09:12,823 --> 00:09:14,850
fewer than one in three do.

289
00:09:15,117 --> 00:09:16,957
Let me draw the doors.

290
00:09:16,957 --> 00:09:19,288
The most common is a workplace retirement

291
00:09:19,288 --> 00:09:19,851
account,

292
00:09:19,851 --> 00:09:21,527
like a four-oh-one-k.

293
00:09:21,527 --> 00:09:22,183
Inside,

294
00:09:22,183 --> 00:09:24,198
many people hold index funds,

295
00:09:24,198 --> 00:09:26,235
which own small slices of hundreds of

296
00:09:26,235 --> 00:09:27,967
companies at once.

297
00:09:27,997 --> 00:09:30,367
Some employers offer stock plans.

298
00:09:30,367 --> 00:09:32,788
Employee stock ownership plans alone cover

299
00:09:32,788 --> 00:09:34,489
millions of people.

300
00:09:34,750 --> 00:09:37,350
But owning isn't free money.

301
00:09:37,350 --> 00:09:39,565
Stock prices can fall hard and take

302
00:09:39,565 --> 00:09:41,550
years to recover.

303
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:43,720
Fees eat returns.

304
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:45,940
And betting your savings on your own

305
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:46,648
employer,

306
00:09:46,648 --> 00:09:49,068
or on a single hot company,

307
00:09:49,068 --> 00:09:50,890
piles risk on risk.

308
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:53,549
Owning a slice of the machines means

309
00:09:53,549 --> 00:09:55,648
sharing their bad years too.

310
00:09:55,883 --> 00:09:56,903
As for Dana,

311
00:09:56,903 --> 00:09:59,194
she finally reads the benefits papers

312
00:09:59,194 --> 00:10:00,963
she'd ignored for years.

313
00:10:00,963 --> 00:10:03,022
Her employer offers a retirement plan,

314
00:10:03,022 --> 00:10:04,723
and she never signed up.

315
00:10:04,723 --> 00:10:05,729
That's her decision,

316
00:10:05,729 --> 00:10:07,303
not advice for anyone else.

317
00:10:07,303 --> 00:10:09,398
But it's the moment she realizes she

318
00:10:09,398 --> 00:10:11,074
could stand on both sides of the

319
00:10:11,074 --> 00:10:11,803
loop.

320
00:10:11,803 --> 00:10:12,932
Earning a paycheck,

321
00:10:12,932 --> 00:10:14,463
and owning a small slice of the

322
00:10:14,463 --> 00:10:15,637
machines.

323
00:10:20,083 --> 00:10:20,512
So,

324
00:10:20,512 --> 00:10:22,317
who gets rich when robots do the

325
00:10:22,317 --> 00:10:23,143
work?

326
00:10:23,223 --> 00:10:24,654
On the evidence so far,

327
00:10:24,654 --> 00:10:26,663
mostly the people who own them.

328
00:10:26,663 --> 00:10:28,692
The shareholders of the companies that

329
00:10:28,692 --> 00:10:29,042
build,

330
00:10:29,042 --> 00:10:30,903
sell and run the machines.

331
00:10:30,903 --> 00:10:32,154
And in America,

332
00:10:32,154 --> 00:10:33,604
that's a group where the richest tenth

333
00:10:33,604 --> 00:10:35,655
hold nearly the whole pile.

334
00:10:35,917 --> 00:10:37,861
But it doesn't have to stay that

335
00:10:37,861 --> 00:10:39,017
narrow.

336
00:10:39,117 --> 00:10:40,903
History says pay can catch up,

337
00:10:40,903 --> 00:10:42,507
after a delay.

338
00:10:42,717 --> 00:10:44,607
New jobs do appear.

339
00:10:44,607 --> 00:10:46,977
And ownership isn't a closed club.

340
00:10:47,117 --> 00:10:49,290
more than half of American families

341
00:10:49,290 --> 00:10:50,653
already own some stock,

342
00:10:50,653 --> 00:10:53,031
even if most own very little.

343
00:10:53,283 --> 00:10:54,533
For generations,

344
00:10:54,533 --> 00:10:56,208
the big money question was,

345
00:10:56,208 --> 00:10:58,503
what do you do for a living?

346
00:10:58,633 --> 00:10:59,793
Dana's question now has a second part.

347
00:10:59,793 --> 00:11:01,709
What do you own?

348
00:11:01,950 --> 00:11:02,557
Next time,

349
00:11:02,557 --> 00:11:05,276
we follow the money behind another hidden

350
00:11:05,276 --> 00:11:07,540
link of the world economy.

351
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:10,283
This is The Hidden Economics.
